    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Development of the human cell lineages has been the subject of much study since the first derivation of human embryonic stem cells (hESCs) and induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs). These cell types can be directed to form early endoderm, mesoderm, ectoderm, trophectoderm, as well as germ cells. Factors such as bone morphogenetic proteins (BMPs) 4 and 8 can differentiate iPSCs to primordial germ cells (PGCs) as well as other lineages such as mesoderm and trophoblast. To address the effect of BMPs and Retinoic Acid (RA) on directed differentiation of female iPSCs, we conducted a 14-day directed differentiation experiment using four treatments and examined the formation of ectoderm, endoderm, pluripotency, germ cell, and trophoblast/mesoderm cell types through analysis of gene expression. Based on previous data from the laboratory, it was hypothesized that the presence of RA during directed differentiation of female iPSCs, in particular, would lead to differentiation of trophoblast or mesoderm tissues, preferentially over other fates including primordial germ cells (PGCs), while BMPs would function to maintain pluripotency and germ cell fates. \r\nFollowing directed differentiation, pluripotency, mesoderm, germ cell, and ectoderm associated genes experienced a gradual decrease in expression while trophectoderm and endoderm genes exhibited an increase in expression throughout the 14 days across all treatments. The hypothesis that the addition of RA would induce trophoblast was supported by increased expression of associated markers. However, addition of +RA did not upregulate mesoderm as hypothesized, with +BMP resulting in negative regulation. Similarly, the hypothesis that +BMP treatment would upregulate germ cell fates was not supported, with all treatments resulting in significant downregulation of germ cell related genes. Variations from the hypothesis may be due to timing of exposure and an unknown pathway mechanism, which merit further investigation. It was further determined that 1) neuroectoderm may be the default differentiation pathway; 2) +BMP upregulates endoderm cell markers and downregulates PGC and mesoderm markers; 3) +BMP/RA downregulates ectoderm, mesoderm, and pluripotency markers while upregulating endoderm and trophectoderm markers; 4) +RA downregulates ectoderm and mesoderm markers. Overall, +BMP appeared to have a more potent effect in positively or negatively regulating gene expression, often with amplified effects in combination with +RA",
